WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:03.160
This is warfarin,

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:06.100
one of the world's most
widely prescribed drugs.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:09.044
It's an anticoagulant which inhibits
blood from clotting

00:00:09.044 --> 00:00:11.640
and is used to prevent and treat stroke,

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:13.960
heart attacks and deep vein thrombosis.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:15.740
So it's a pretty big deal,

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:20.400
and it all started with some mouldy hay 
and a load of dead cows.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.480
The story begins in the Midwest USA.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:28.880
During the 1920s dairy farmers were losing 
cow after cow to a strange disease

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.520
that caused their cattle to bleed to death.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.500
Vets soon identified the culprit:

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.400
a crop in the cattle feed called sweet clover.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.920
If it was left to go mouldy and fed to cows

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.440
it seemed to make them sick

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.320
but no one knew why.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:46.480
Unfortunately few could afford alternative feeds,

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:51.440
and so in the winter of 1933 
one desperate farmer, Ed Carlson,

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:56.760
made a 200-mile journey to the University of 
Wisconsin in search of help.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.520
In the back of his truck were a
hundred pounds of sweet clover

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.620
and a milk can full of blood that wouldn't clot.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:05.020
Oh and a dead cow.

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:08.700
Eventually he stumbled upon a 
biochemist called Karl Link

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.470
and made a plea for help

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:14.460
although there was nothing Link could do 
for Carlson's cows.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:18.000
But this chance meeting would have a
profound effect on Link

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.140
and he soon set his team on the hunt

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:23.400
for the chemical responsible 
for the sweet clover disease.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.220
After seven years of research they found it:

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:30.440
a compound called dicoumarol which
inhibited blood clotting

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:33.260
in a number of animals such as mice and rabbits.

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:36.660
In fact one variant was so potent in rats

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.840
that they developed it into a rat poison

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.640
and called it warfarin.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:43.680
Unfortunately for rats, it became one of

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.720
the most popular rodenticides on the market.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:50.660
Soon research showed that warfarin could be 
used safely in humans

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:53.700
but few doctors wanted to prescribe a rat poison.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:56.579
So when it was approved for
clinical use in 1954

00:01:56.579 --> 00:01:59.980
it was sold under a different name: Coumadin.

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:04.290
In 1955 it was used to treat President Dwight Eisenhower following a heart attack,

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:07.120
which helped boost its popularity further.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:10.600
After all, if it was good enough for the most powerful man in the world,

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.380
it was good enough for
everyone else.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:18.920
Fast forward 50 years and other
anticoagulant drugs have been developed

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:22.120
but warfarin is still used to treat
millions of people.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:26.600
In fact it remains on the World Health Organization's 
list of essential medicines.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.340
And all of this down to a chance meeting

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:33.860
between a dairy farmer 
and a determined biochemist.

